  and `opened_other.jsonl` (every file opened, with its time), `no_number_keys.json.gz`, the first-opening printout
  `r10_open_official.out.txt`, and the small exploration scripts `r00`-`r40`.

## Lead's addendum (2026-11-16)

Written by the lead after reading the round's return at 19:22 local time: the reconciler's output, the implementers'
summaries and the three skeptics' reports. Before 19:22 the lead had read no comparison statistic of this round. The
tallies below come from the final `mismatches.json` (18:49:50) and from A's and B's `results.json`; the skeptics'
files are in `V\skeptic\blindness`, `V\skeptic\numbers` and `V\skeptic\reconcile`.

### A1. Corrections to the reconciler's text

1. Section 1: the runs of `reconcile.py` re-read the compare files from 18:34:26 to 18:49:41 (the final run included;
   360 opens, plus 16 reads of `pairs.parquet` as bytes for its SHA-256), not to 18:46:03.
2. Sections 6 and 7: `work\tables.md` was not written; `work\r30_tables.py` prints its tables and keeps no file. The
   tables are in sections 3 and 6 of this file, and every B1 number with its interval is in A's and B's
   `results.json` under `b1`.
3. Group 2 (the sign agreement's interval) does touch what S3.13 prints. Section 10 asks S3.13 for "pairs x statistics
   with intervals", and the official files hold no sign-agreement interval. Their `finite_replicates` = 0 is right
   only on the 7 pair-levels where no sign is determined in both models on any draw; on the other 95 pair-levels no
   draw was evaluated, so that 0 must not be printed. The intervals that exist are A's (= B's).
4. Group 3 (a family with no variable): section 8's rule (a family's attribution is the sum of its variables' phi, and
   an empty sum is 0) already gives compare_shap's 0. The main row and S3.13 should say "no own-history input" for
   coral and "no context or calendar input" for defo, not print 0.00 [0.00, 0.00] or 0.0%.
5. Group 8 ("the median rival"): NDVI has four rivals, so its value 0.2560 [0.2357, 0.2757] is the median of four
   shares (the mean of the two middle ones), and no rival has it. Its column should be labelled "median of the
   rivals", not "median rival".
6. Groups 4 and 5 (the feature-level family-share distance): section 8 lists the family-share distance among the
   statistics of "every pair, both levels", and section 10 asks for the B1 pair's distance between the two runs'
   family shares at both levels, so the words can also be read as settling the question. Under section 8's
   definition the families are sums of variable-level phi, so a feature-level family distance would repeat the
   variable-level value (B's reading); no separate feature-level family share is defined. Whether the feature-level
   tables repeat the value or say "variable level only" is for the owner (A5).
7. Group 16 (test-set columns): A's extra columns are the context columns and the own-history `x__hist_*` columns
   (defo: A lists 310 columns and B 306; only in A: `x__hist_last_1` to `x__hist_last_3`, `x__hist_mean_5` and
   `x__hist_mean_all`; only in B: `issue`). That both read the same records is checked in A3.
8. Two latent deviations of `compare_shap.py`, with no effect here: item 8's top 10 is ordered by `np.argsort(-Iz)`,
   not by section 8's name tie rule (no tie occurred: the 12 largest item-8 shares are distinct for all five
   arcsine-link models); and the AW3 header lists at most 30 structural zeros (the largest count at any level is 29,
   defo's feature level).
9. Two latent weaknesses of `reconcile.py`, with no effect here: `classify()` puts a key into a group by its pattern
   and by what the official files lack, without looking at the A-against-B status (A2 shows that every key of the
   groups whose diagnosis says A = B has AB = match); and `verdict()` does not count the official-derived statuses
   AOd and BOd as mismatches (all 921 derived keys are present in A and B: 916 match, and the 5 that differ are the
   item-8 J keys of group 9).

