### A4. The three skeptics

**Blindness and discipline: ready, three minor findings.**

1. The spec writer twice started Python without `-I -B`: a package check that printed versions and a
   ModuleNotFoundError for jsonschema. It opened no project file.
2. The spec writer's `inspect_structure.py` printed, besides `per_lead.json`'s structural keys, the number of its other
   top-level keys (13 per problem), after walking the key tree in memory. No other key name or value was printed.
3. PROTOCOL.md section 14 (lines 771-777) describes what the earlier official fire comparison wrote in its AW4 files
   (W undefined and the other cells empty; no statistic), and both A and B read that passage. Both followed brief
   item 7 instead and report fire treeroute's top 3 and own-history share, so no open choice was steered.

It also found that A never opened `V\B` and B never opened `V\A`; that no SHAP script was run; that every Python start
by A and B used `-I -B`; that no compare file other than `per_lead.json` (structural keys) and `bootstrap_draws.npy`
was opened before the reconciler's first opening at 18:24:54; and that, by its scan at 18:33, no file in SHAP outside
V had been created or changed from 17:09 on. The lead's own scan at 19:30, after the round, found the same for the
whole round: of 10,801 files (V and `smoke\bardl_fix` not entered), none was created or changed at or after 17:09; the
newest is `RUN_COMMANDS.md` (17:05:41).

**The numbers: ready, three minor findings.**

1. NDVI's pairs with BARDL. BARDL gives exactly 0 to 93 of the 96 variables of J, so its top-5, top-10 and top-20 sets
   are filled by name order (section 8's tie rule; code-point order puts `aet_total_mm` first, a variable BARDL does
   not use). The BARDL pairs' Jaccard, RBO and top-20-union Spearman therefore depend on that rule. For
   rival_bardl_h1|treeroute at the variable level, Jaccard top 5 is 0.25 with `aet_total_mm` and 0.11 without it
   (top 10: 0.25 against 0.18). A, B and the official files share these numbers. The main-text rows are not affected:
   under three collations no main-row number moves by more than 3e-5. No other pair, in any problem or at either
   level, has a top-k overlap that exists only through a tie.
2. This report was missing (it is written now).
3. PM2.5's RBO against the consensus, 0.3720 [0.3645, 0.5707], lies at the low edge of its interval because its
   bootstrap distribution has two peaks. The consensus top 3 lie within 0.0035 of each other (spi 0.0813,
   sst_anom_global_tropical 0.0791, hist_last 0.0778); on draws where hist_last, treeroute's top variable, becomes the
   consensus's top variable, the RBO jumps. 91.1% of the draws lie above the full-sample value.

With its own code, written from the protocol's words, it recomputed the five main rows, a sample of pairs, coral
treeroute's importances, defo's identity and the fire, pm25 and ndvi B1 numbers; every one equals A, B and the
official files (largest difference 1.5e-15). It regenerated the five draw matrices from section 3's generator and
section 8's salt (all equal to `bootstrap_draws.npy`) and checked the SHA-256 of all 29 row files against their
records.

**The reconciliation: not ready, one major and eight minor findings.** The major finding was that this report did not
exist; it is written now. The eight minor findings are A1 items 3 to 9. The skeptic also re-ran `reconcile.py` in a
copy (`V\skeptic\reconcile\rerun`): `mismatches.json` and `summary.json` came out equal in every field but the run
times, and its own mapping of every official row agreed within 1e-12 apart from the known groups.

### A5. Points for the owner

1. Numbers the protocol asks for but `compare_shap.py` did not write: fire's main-row treeroute top 3 and own-history
   share (group 1), and the sign-agreement intervals (group 2). A and B computed them and agree, and their full-sample
   values equal the official ones. Proposed: S3.13 and the main row take them from A (= B) and say so.
2. The feature-level family-share distance (A1 item 6). Proposed: report it at the variable level only and say that
   the feature level would repeat the same value.
3. NDVI's BARDL pair cells (A4, the numbers, item 1). Proposed: keep them, with a footnote on the name-order ties.
4. Presentation, proposed unless the owner objects: "no own-history input" (coral) and "no context or calendar input"
   (defo) instead of 0 (A1 item 4); "median of the rivals" for NDVI's four rivals (A1 item 5); a one-line note on
   PM2.5's asymmetric RBO interval (A4).
